    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The solenoids look okay on the scan but the pressure spikes are still erratic. Could there be something more subtle, like internal leakages or maybe something physically blocking the passages in the valve body? I'm trying to figure out if I need to pull the whole unit or if it's just a component swap.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, subtle internal leakages or restricted passages in the valve body are very likely causes when solenoids appear fine. These issues create unpredictable pressure drops and spikes as fluid bypasses components prematurely. Before deciding on a full unit replacement, closely inspecting the valve body for debris or evidence of sticking passages is a smart intermediate step. This detailed inspection can often pinpoint the exact restricted area without tearing the entire transmission apart.",
      "source": "target",
